WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.962
Authentication answers who is making the
request.

2
00:00:06.042 --> 00:00:13.664
Next.js applications may use session
cookies, token-based sessions,

3
00:00:13.744 --> 00:00:21.873
passwordless sign-in, federated identity,
MFA, step-up authentication,

4
00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:25.637
or a dedicated identity provider.

5
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:33.974
The implementation details vary, but the
security questions remain stable.

6
00:00:34.054 --> 00:00:37.229
How is identity established?

7
00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:40.739
How is session state protected?

8
00:00:40.819 --> 00:00:43.233
How is logout handled?

9
00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:46.869
How are tokens or cookies stored?

10
00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:52.411
How does the server know the request is
still valid?

11
00:00:52.491 --> 00:01:00.113
Cookies need careful handling when they
represent session authority.

12
00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:08.323
HttpOnly helps keep session cookies out of
normal client-side JavaScript.

13
00:01:08.403 --> 00:01:13.484
Secure limits transmission to HTTPS
contexts.

14
00:01:13.564 --> 00:01:19.534
SameSite can reduce some cross-site
request behavior.

15
00:01:19.614 --> 00:01:25.838
Domain and path settings control where the
cookie is sent.

16
00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:32.015
Expiration and renewal policy affect
session lifetime.

17
00:01:32.095 --> 00:01:36.287
These attributes are not decorations;

18
00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:44.243
they are part of the security model for
browser-authenticated requests.

19
00:01:44.323 --> 00:01:51.690
Authentication success does not
automatically grant broad access.

20
00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:57.995
A user may be signed in but not allowed to
read every record,

21
00:01:58.075 --> 00:02:06.585
edit every organization, invoke every API
handler, or perform every mutation.

22
00:02:06.665 --> 00:02:13.271
API keys and service credentials can
identify integrations,

23
00:02:13.351 --> 00:02:19.702
but they do not automatically prove an end
user's identity.

24
00:02:19.782 --> 00:02:28.293
Session state, token validation, issuer,
audience, expiration, revocation,

25
00:02:28.373 --> 00:02:35.232
and intended use all need to line up with
the application design.

26
00:02:35.312 --> 00:02:42.172
Session behavior crosses server, browser,
and API boundaries.

27
00:02:42.252 --> 00:02:49.873
Account recovery, password reset, MFA
changes, role changes, logout,

28
00:02:49.953 --> 00:02:59.099
and session invalidation can affect
whether existing sessions should remain valid.

29
00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:07.817
A secure application treats authentication
as a lifecycle, not a one-time flag.

30
00:03:07.897 --> 00:03:15.010
It also avoids logging tokens, placing
secrets in client bundles,

31
00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:23.220
or exposing session internals through
error messages or debugging output.
